<article class="jd-rulebook">
  <header class="jd-rulebook__title">
    <span class="jd-kicker">Ghost stories in the manner of M. R. James</span>
    <h1>Jamesian Dark</h1>
    <p>A hack of Graham Walmsley's <em>Cthulhu Dark</em>. You play bookish people who disturb something that should have been left alone. You cannot defeat it; you can find out what it is, try to lay it to rest, or get away.</p>
  </header>

  <h2>Making an Investigator</h2>
  <p>Choose a name, an occupation and an area of scholarly interest: the thing that gets you into trouble. Notice starts at 1. Play works best with one to three Investigators.</p>

  <h2>The Outcome roll</h2>
  <p>Roll only when the result is uncertain and either failure or a cost would be interesting. If you can simply do something, you do it. Otherwise build a pool from every die that applies, and read the highest die, of any colour.</p>
  <ul class="jd-rulebook__dice">
    <li><span class="jd-die jd-die--light" data-face="1"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></span> One Light die if the action is within human capability. Almost every action is.</li>
    <li><span class="jd-die jd-die--scholar" data-face="1"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></span> One Light die if it falls within your occupation or scholarly interest.</li>
    <li><span class="jd-die jd-die--notice" data-face="1"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></span> The Notice die if you risk your mind: facing, handling or concentrating on something supernatural, or pressing on while frightened. You may also add it by choice. Never at Notice 6.</li>
    <li><span class="jd-die jd-die--meddle" data-face="1"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></span> The Meddle die if you meddle.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="jd-rulebook__tables">
    <table>
      <caption>Investigating</caption>
      <tr><th>1–3</th><td>The bare minimum: what you need to go on, and nothing more.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>4</th><td>Everything a competent scholar would find.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>5</th><td>More: a parallel case, a remembered footnote, a local story.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>6</th><td>A glimpse of what is really happening. Make a Notice roll.</td></tr>
    </table>
    <table>
      <caption>Doing anything else</caption>
      <tr><th>1–3</th><td>You choose: fail, or succeed at a cost the GM names.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>4</th><td>Success.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>5</th><td>Success, done well.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>6</th><td>Success, and you notice something wrong. The GM may call for a Notice roll.</td></tr>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p><strong>Triggers.</strong> After reading the result, check the special dice in this order:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Meddle die highest, or tied for highest: Notice rises by 1 automatically.</li>
    <li>Notice die strictly higher than every other die: make a Notice roll.</li>
    <li>A 6 while investigating: make a Notice roll.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The second and third never stack: one Outcome roll causes at most one Notice roll. The first always applies on top. A tie between the Notice die and a Light die does not trigger a Notice roll.</p>

  <h2>Notice</h2>
  <p>Notice runs from 1 to 6 and measures how far the thing has noticed you. It is the only track in the game: it decides how close the thing comes, and how your story ends.</p>
  <p><strong>The Notice roll.</strong> Roll one die. If it is higher than your current Notice, Notice rises by 1. Otherwise nothing happens.</p>
  <p>The GM calls for a Notice roll when you witness something wrong: a manifestation, a trace, an object behaving impossibly. One event means one Notice roll per witness; a sound that goes on all night is a single event. Mundane danger, however grim, does not call for one.</p>
  <p>Notice also rises without a roll when the Meddle die is highest or tied for highest (by 1), and when you try to fight the thing (straight to 6). It falls only through Leaving.</p>
  <p><strong>At Notice 6</strong> the thing has already noticed you. You make no more Notice rolls and set the Notice die aside for good: it is never added to any roll again, for any reason. Because trying again depends on the Notice die, you can no longer try again either. The Meddle die can no longer raise your Notice, so meddling at 6 costs nothing more; the worst has already happened.</p>

  <h2>Meddling</h2>
  <p>Meddling is a transgression: the tempting shortcut every James scholar takes. Declare the act, or accept a bargain the GM offers, and add the Meddle die to your roll. It makes success likelier, and it may be what gives you away.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>If the Meddle die is highest, or tied for highest, Notice rises by 1 automatically.</li>
    <li>If any other die beats it, the meddling went unremarked.</li>
    <li>If the Meddle die and the Notice die tie for highest, apply both: Notice rises by 1, then make a Notice roll.</li>
    <li>Each transgression counts once. Prising up a stone is one act of meddling; carrying on reading the inscription under it is not another.</li>
    <li>Meddling always involves a roll. If the act would not normally need one, the GM calls for one anyway, so that the Meddle die is rolled.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>What counts as meddling</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Taking something away from where it was left.</li>
    <li>Reading words aloud, or using an object for its purpose.</li>
    <li>Opening what was sealed, or disturbing the dead.</li>
    <li>Going where you were warned not to, or ignoring a warning.</li>
    <li>Sleeping in, staying in, or returning to the wrong place.</li>
    <li>Making a likeness of the cursed thing, where the scenario says likenesses matter.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Restitution is never meddling.</strong> Returning the object, completing the interrupted rite or restoring what was disturbed does not use the Meddle die, even if it means handling the cursed thing.</p>
  <p><strong>Keeping it legible.</strong> The GM offers bargains: “You could prise the lid off. That's meddling.” You may always ask whether an act counts, and the GM answers honestly, every time.</p>

  <h2>Failing and trying again</h2>
  <p><strong>The Failure Die.</strong> After any roll for Doing anything else, anyone at the table may propose that you fail instead. They describe how, and roll one die. If it is higher than your highest die, you fail exactly as described; if it is equal or lower, the roll stands. It can be proposed once per roll, never against Investigating, and never against the Visit roll: the Visit is already the test. If you had chosen to succeed at a cost and the Failure Die then succeeds, the cost is cancelled and you simply fail.</p>
  <p><strong>Trying again.</strong> After any roll, including one the Failure Die has overturned, you may try again. If the Notice die was not in the pool, add it, then reroll every die. If it was, first make any Notice roll the current result has triggered, then reroll every die. Each attempt is a fresh roll with its own triggers, and rerolling never cancels anything the earlier roll triggered. At Notice 6 you cannot try again, because you can neither add the Notice die nor make a Notice roll. The one exception is meddling: an act of meddling can raise Notice only once, however many times the roll is retried.</p>

  <h2>Working together</h2>
  <p>When two or more Investigators work on the same task, and can plausibly help each other, make one group roll. The GM decides how many can usefully help.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Each helper builds and rolls their own pool as normal.</li>
    <li>The group's result is the highest die rolled by anyone.</li>
    <li>Each helper checks the triggers against their own dice only. While investigating, every helper who rolled a 6 makes a Notice roll. As with any roll, no helper makes more than one Notice roll from it.</li>
    <li><strong>Meddling together.</strong> If the task is a transgression, everyone who helps is meddling and adds their Meddle die. You may stand by instead: keeping watch, holding the lamp. Standing by adds no dice and is not meddling, but nobody present is alone.</li>
    <li><strong>Costs and failure.</strong> On a 1–3 doing anything else, the players choose together whether to fail or succeed at a cost; if they can't agree, they fail. A Failure Die must beat the group's highest die; if it does, the whole group fails.</li>
    <li><strong>Trying again.</strong> The group rerolls together. A helper unwilling or unable to add the Notice die, or to make a Notice roll already due, such as one at Notice 6, drops out first.</li>
    <li><strong>Competing.</strong> Each rolls their own pool and the highest die wins. On a tie, both reroll.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>At this table, each helper rolls from their own sheet. Read the best card, and propose the Failure Die on that one.</p>

  <h2>Solitude and the Visit</h2>
  <p><strong>Alone</strong> means no other conscious person within sight and earshot. A sleeping companion does not count. A stranger in the same room does.</p>
  <p>Atmosphere and traces can reach anyone. The thing manifests directly, close at hand or by touch, only to someone who is alone. Company is always safe from direct manifestation.</p>
  <p><strong>The Visit.</strong> At Notice 6, the thing comes for you the next time you are alone. If you are alone when you reach 6, it comes at once.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Play a short scene. You cannot win. You may run, hide or endure, with a Doing anything else roll: a Light die, plus a second if your occupation or scholarly interest genuinely helps. The Notice die is set aside at 6. Nobody may propose the Failure Die against this roll, and it cannot be tried again.</li>
    <li>On a 4 or more, you survive: you reach company, or dawn comes.</li>
    <li>On 1–3, you may choose to survive at a cost the GM names, unless you struck at the thing. If you choose to fail, you and the GM decide together what remains of you, and play it out in the epilogue.</li>
    <li>From now on your epilogue is set by how you survived, not by your Notice: on a 4 or more, Never quite well again; at a cost, Ruined.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>You suffer the Visit at most once. Afterwards you may keep playing. The thing can still reach you as at Notice 5 (proximity, when alone), but it never Visits you again. Once the thing is laid to rest, no further Visits happen.</p>

  <h2>Fighting, leaving and laying it to rest</h2>
  <p><strong>Fighting.</strong> The thing can be struck at only when it shows itself: as a far-off figure, which anyone may see, or close at hand to someone alone at Notice 5. Any attempt to harm, restrain or drive it off by force achieves nothing, and your Notice goes straight to 6. If you are alone, the Visit begins at once; if not, it comes the next time you are alone. Destroying or defacing the cursed object is never fighting: it is meddling or restitution, as the scenario says. Running past the thing, hiding from it or holding a door shut is not fighting either: roll Doing anything else with the Notice die (unless you are at Notice 6). Mundane threats are resolved normally.</p>
  <p><strong>Fighting during the Visit.</strong> You may still strike at the thing during the Visit. Roll as enduring, but on 1–3 you cannot choose to survive at a cost: you do not survive, and you and the GM decide together what remains of you.</p>
  <p><strong>Leaving.</strong> You may leave the story at any time by doing something decisive: fleeing the district, burning your notes, swearing never to speak of it. Roll one die; if it is lower than your Notice, Notice falls by 1. You take no further part and go straight to your epilogue. Leaving does not avoid a Visit already due: at Notice 6, if you leave alone you meet the thing on the way. To leave safely, leave in company.</p>
  <p><strong>Laying it to rest.</strong> Each scenario defines its restitution: returning the object, finishing the rite, burning the likeness on the stone. Attempting it is a Doing anything else roll, usually with the Notice die (except for anyone at Notice 6). On 1–3 the players choose as usual: the restitution does not take this time and must be attempted again later, or it takes at a cost the GM names. When it succeeds, the threat ends at once, no further Notice rolls or Visits happen, and each epilogue is set by current Notice.</p>

  <h2>Epilogues</h2>
  <p>The story ends when the thing is laid to rest, when every Investigator has left, or when the scenario's time runs out. Your Notice at that moment sets your epilogue.</p>
  <table class="jd-rulebook__epilogue">
    <tr><th>1–2</th><td>Untouched. Perhaps a little quieter at dinner for a while.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>3–4</th><td>Never quite well again. Will not discuss it; keeps a light burning; avoids certain places or things.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>5–6</th><td>Ruined. Gone abroad, hair turned white, or found in the morning.</td></tr>
  </table>
  <ul>
    <li>Anyone who suffered the Visit has their band set by it, whatever their Notice and even if it later falls through Leaving: Never quite well again if they survived on a 4 or more, Ruined if they survived at a cost.</li>
    <li>A scenario may move you one band worse for a loose end, such as a likeness never destroyed. Nobody goes beyond the worst band.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>You choose what your band looks like for your Investigator; the GM may suggest a detail from play. Keep it to one or two sentences.</p>

  <h2>Rulings at a glance</h2>
  <table class="jd-rulebook__rulings">
    <tr><th>Notice die ties a Light die for highest</th><td>No Notice roll.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Notice die is highest and shows 6 while investigating</th><td>One Notice roll, not two.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Meddle die ties for highest</th><td>Notice rises by 1.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Meddle die and Notice die tie for highest</th><td>Notice rises by 1, then one Notice roll.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Retrying a roll that included meddling</th><td>The meddling can raise Notice only once per act.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Meddling act that needs no roll</th><td>Call for a roll anyway, so the Meddle die is rolled.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Meddling nobody declared or asked about</th><td>No Meddle die. Any consequence comes from the scenario.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Returning or restoring the cursed thing</th><td>Not meddling.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Failure Die against Investigating</th><td>Not allowed.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Failure Die succeeds after a cost was accepted</th><td>The cost is cancelled; the Investigator fails.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Several Investigators help with one task</th><td>One group roll. The result is the highest die anyone rolled.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Triggers in a group roll</th><td>Each helper checks their own dice only. Anyone who rolled a 6 while investigating makes a Notice roll.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Helping with a transgression</th><td>Every helper is meddling. Standing by is not.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Failure Die against a group roll</th><td>It must beat the group's highest die. If it does, everyone fails.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>The same manifestation continues for hours</th><td>One Notice roll per witness.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Investigator at Notice 6</th><td>No more Notice rolls, no Notice die, no trying again. The Meddle die cannot raise Notice.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Companion is asleep</th><td>The Investigator counts as alone.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Reaching Notice 6 while alone</th><td>The Visit happens at once.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Laid to rest before an Investigator at 6 is alone</th><td>No Visit; their epilogue uses Notice 6.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Leaving alone at Notice 6</th><td>The Visit comes on the way.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Failure Die against the Visit roll</th><td>Not allowed.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>A Visit survivor is alone again</th><td>The thing can reach them as at Notice 5. No second Visit.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>A Visit survivor later leaves</th><td>Their band is still set by how they survived the Visit.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Epilogue for a Visit survivor</th><td>Survived on a 4 or more: Never quite well again. Survived at a cost: Ruined.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Laying it to rest on a 1–3</th><td>Players choose: it fails for now, or it takes at a cost.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Holding a door, running past, hiding</th><td>Not fighting. Roll with the Notice die, unless at Notice 6.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Striking at the thing</th><td>Notice goes to 6. If alone, the Visit begins at once.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Striking at the thing during the Visit</th><td>Roll as enduring. On 1–3, no surviving at a cost.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Destroying or defacing the cursed object</th><td>Not fighting. Meddling or restitution, as the scenario says.</td></tr>
  </table>

  {{#if isGM}}
  <h2>For the GM</h2>
  <p>Four principles guide every ruling. Curiosity is tempting and costly. The thing comes to those who are alone. You cannot win a fight with it. Endings are diminished, not triumphant.</p>

  <h3>Escalation</h3>
  <p>Each Investigator's Notice sets the most the thing can do to them.</p>
  <table class="jd-rulebook__epilogue">
    <tr><th>1–2</th><td>Atmosphere: sounds in another room, smells, a distant figure, objects subtly moved.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>3–4</th><td>Traces: marks, smears, footprints, the cursed object changing.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>5</th><td>Proximity, only when alone: a hand on the latch, something keeping pace, a touch in the dark.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>6</th><td>The Visit, the next time they are alone.</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>Each night, the thing reaches the Investigator with the highest Notice, at the level their Notice allows. If two are tied, choose the one who has handled the cursed object most; if still tied, you choose. You may also give atmosphere to anyone else.</p>

  <h3>Meddling nobody asked about</h3>
  <p>You need not volunteer that an act is meddling. If a player does something that counts without asking and without being offered it as a bargain, no Meddle die is rolled; any consequence arrives later, through the fiction, as the scenario describes. Use this at most once per scenario, and only where the scenario plans for it.</p>

  <h3>Group rolls and the Failure Die</h3>
  <p>You may lay the cost of a group roll on any helper. You may refuse a Failure Die proposal that would simply stall the scene.</p>

  <h3>Running the game</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>One transgression, one thing.</strong> Build each story around a transgression already made and a single entity tethered to an object or place.</li>
    <li><strong>Seed warnings.</strong> An inscription, a sexton's remark, a note in a margin, locals who won't go up the lane. Point to them freely. Ignoring a warning is meddling, and players should be able to see that.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep every essential clue safe.</strong> Six Provenance discoveries: letters, registers, engravings, a diary, the object itself. Each piece of information the Investigators need must be reachable without meddling; meddling should get them there faster, or further, never be the only way.</li>
    <li><strong>Offer bargains.</strong> When the table stalls, name a shortcut and the fact that it is meddling. Players choosing doom with their eyes open is the heart of the game.</li>
    <li><strong>Control the pace with solitude.</strong> If Notice is stalling and the thing barely matters, give someone a reason to be alone: an errand across the marsh at dusk, a forgotten book in the vestry, a room of their own. Or offer a bargain. If Notice is climbing fast, let the Investigators have warm scenes together: tea, a fire, a fussy argument about dates. The comfort makes the next horror land harder.</li>
    <li><strong>Escalate slowly.</strong> Unease (a small wrongness), dread (something specific), terror (alone, and it is near), horror (the thing in the room, once per story at most). After the horror, return to quiet: a diminished coda, not a climax.</li>
    <li><strong>Stay reticent.</strong> Never describe the thing whole. Give one detail at a time: a hand, damp linen, hair, the wrong number of limbs felt in the dark.</li>
    <li><strong>Loose ends.</strong> If the scenario has one (an image not destroyed, a bead not returned), it says what that costs.</li>
    <li><strong>Optional frame.</strong> Open with a narrator, years later: “My friend never spoke of what happened at Burnstow…” If you opened with a narrator, close with the narrator too.</li>
  </ul>
  {{/if}}
